      html: `
<p class="lead">In the 19th century, the great powers decided Luxembourg's fate. Step by step it became a <b>sovereign, neutral State</b>.</p>

<div class="timeline">
  <div><b>1815</b><span><b>Congress of Vienna</b>: Luxembourg becomes a <b>Grand Duchy</b>, given to <b>King William I of the Netherlands</b> (House of Orange-Nassau) in <b>personal union</b>. It is also a member of the <b>German Confederation</b>, with a <b>Prussian garrison</b> in the fortress. Territory east of the Moselle, Sauer and Our goes to Prussia.</span></div>
  <div><b>1830–1839</b><span>During the <b>Belgian Revolution</b>, most Luxembourgers side with Belgium.</span></div>
  <div><b>19 April 1839</b><span><b>Treaty of London</b>: <b>independence</b> is recognised, but the western, French-speaking part, <b>almost two-thirds of the territory</b>, goes to Belgium (today's Belgian province of Luxembourg). This is the "third partition". The country stays in the German Confederation, ruled by the Dutch king. <b>1839 is considered the birth of the independent State.</b></span></div>
  <div><b>1841</b><span>First constitution (King-Grand Duke <b>William II</b>): an "assembly of estates" elected by restricted (property-based) vote.</span></div>
  <div><b>1848</b><span>The revolutions across Europe reach Luxembourg. A <b>liberal constitution</b> on the Belgian model is adopted, <b>press censorship is abolished</b>, and elected communal councils are created.</span></div>
  <div><b>1856</b><span><b>William III</b> stages a coup and imposes a reactionary constitution.</span></div>
  <div><b>1868</b><span>A compromise constitution: the basis of the Constitution until 2023.</span></div>
  <div><b>1842</b><span>Luxembourg joins the German customs union, the <b>Zollverein</b> (until 1918).</span></div>
  <div><b>1859</b><span>The <b>first railway line</b> opens, ending the country's isolation.</span></div>
  <div><b>1859</b><span><b>"Ons Heemecht"</b>: lyrics by <b>Michel Lentz</b>, music by <b>Jean-Antoine Zinnen</b> (first sung in 1864). It later becomes the national anthem.</span></div>
  <div><b>11 May 1867</b><span><b>Second Treaty of London</b>, after the "<b>Luxembourg Crisis</b>": <b>Napoleon III</b> wanted to buy the Grand Duchy, King-Grand Duke William III agreed to sell it for 5 million guilders, and <b>Bismarck</b> (Prussia) refused. The treaty gives Luxembourg <b>perpetual neutrality</b>, the Prussian garrison leaves, and the <b>fortress is dismantled</b>. Luxembourg also leaves the German Confederation.</span></div>
  <div><b>1890</b><span>King William III dies without a male heir. Wilhelmina becomes queen of the Netherlands, but under the Nassau family pact only a man could inherit Luxembourg. The <b>personal union ends</b>, and <b>Adolphe of Nassau-Weilburg</b> becomes Grand Duke. Since then, Luxembourg has had <b>its own dynasty</b>.</span></div>
</div>

<h3>The three partitions</h3>
<ol>
  <li><b>1659</b>: Treaty of the Pyrenees, the south (Thionville area) goes to France.</li>
  <li><b>1815</b>: Congress of Vienna, the territory east of the Moselle, Sauer and Our goes to Prussia.</li>
  <li><b>1839</b>: Treaty of London, the western part (about two-thirds) goes to Belgium.</li>
</ol>
`,
