      html: `
<p class="lead">The European Union has <b>27 member states</b> (since the UK left in 2020). It works through institutions that each have a clear role, and several of them sit in <b>Luxembourg</b>.</p>

<div class="grid2">
  <div class="mini"><h4><b>European Parliament</b></h4><p>Elected directly every 5 years (since 1979); <b>720 MEPs</b>, of which <b>6 from Luxembourg</b>. It adopts EU laws and the budget with the Council, and controls the Commission.</p><p style="margin-top:6px">📍 <b>Strasbourg</b> (plenary sessions), Brussels; <b>General Secretariat in Luxembourg</b></p></div>
  <div class="mini"><h4><b>European Council</b></h4><p>The <b>heads of State or government</b> and its President. Sets the general political direction; it does not make laws.</p><p style="margin-top:6px">📍 Brussels</p></div>
  <div class="mini"><h4><b>Council of the EU</b> ("Council of Ministers")</h4><p>National <b>ministers</b>; adopts laws and the budget with Parliament. Its presidency <b>rotates every 6 months</b>.</p><p style="margin-top:6px">📍 Brussels; meetings in <b>Luxembourg in April, June and October</b></p></div>
  <div class="mini"><h4><b>European Commission</b></h4><p><b>One commissioner per member state</b>. Has the (almost exclusive) <b>right to propose laws</b>, implements policies and is the "guardian of the treaties".</p><p style="margin-top:6px">📍 Brussels (some services, e.g. <b>Eurostat</b>, in Luxembourg)</p></div>
  <div class="mini"><h4><b>Court of Justice of the EU</b></h4><p>Ensures EU law is <b>interpreted and applied uniformly</b>; one judge per member state; includes the <b>General Court</b>.</p><p style="margin-top:6px">📍 <b>Luxembourg</b> (since 1952)</p></div>
  <div class="mini"><h4><b>European Court of Auditors</b></h4><p>Checks that the <b>EU budget</b> is properly spent.</p><p style="margin-top:6px">📍 <b>Luxembourg</b></p></div>
  <div class="mini"><h4><b>European Central Bank</b></h4><p>Manages the <b>euro</b> and monetary policy.</p><p style="margin-top:6px">📍 Frankfurt</p></div>
  <div class="mini"><h4><b>European Investment Bank</b></h4><p>The EU's bank: finances projects.</p><p style="margin-top:6px">📍 <b>Luxembourg</b> (since 1968)</p></div>
</div>

<h3>How an EU law is made (ordinary legislative procedure)</h3>
<div class="steps">
  <div><b>1. Proposal</b><span>The <b>Commission</b> proposes a regulation or directive.</span></div>
  <div><b>2. Co-decision</b><span>The <b>European Parliament</b> and the <b>Council of the EU</b> amend and must both approve it.</span></div>
  <div><b>3. Application</b><span>A <b>regulation</b> applies directly everywhere; a <b>directive</b> sets a goal that each country must put into its own law. Texts are published in the <b>Official Journal of the EU</b> (by the Publications Office in Luxembourg).</span></div>
</div>

<h3>Advisory bodies</h3>
<ul>
  <li><b>European Economic and Social Committee</b> (employers, workers, civil society) and <b>European Committee of the Regions</b> (local and regional authorities), both in Brussels.</li>
</ul>

<h3>EU citizenship (since Maastricht, 1993)</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Freedom to <b>move, live and work</b> anywhere in the EU.</li>
  <li>Right to <b>vote and stand</b> in <b>communal</b> and <b>European</b> elections in the country where you live.</li>
  <li>Consular protection from any EU country outside the EU; right to petition the European Parliament and to complain to the European Ombudsman.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout tip"><b>The euro and Schengen:</b> Luxembourg uses the <b>euro</b> (since 1999/2002, managed by the ECB in Frankfurt) and is in the <b>Schengen area</b> without internal border checks. Its central bank governor sits on the ECB Governing Council.</div>
`,
